{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1692361792/4/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c61be7dc\" lines=\"42-57\">\n<description>\nFlatten the interior object into a centered line and move the guide bars inward to bracket it.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- One non-background guide color touches the boundary and forms exactly two full parallel bars plus a split perpendicular center bar.\n- The other non-background color is a single 8-connected interior object whose area determines the flattened line length.\n\nRule:\n- If the guide pair is vertical, make a vertical object-colored line centered between those bars; otherwise use the analogous horizontal line.\n- The line length equals the number of interior object pixels and is centered on the split perpendicular guide bar.\n- Rebuild the guide color as the perpendicular center bar plus two full bars immediately beside the flattened object line.\n\nKey insight:\n- The guide bars supply only the orientation and center; the interior object's pixel count supplies the length of the final one-cell-thick line.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"21897d95\" lines=\"283-301\">\n<description>\nRepaint regions from T-shaped control markers, erase the markers, and optionally rotate based on a color-9 L-marker.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is a mosaic of monochrome regions.\n- Color 1 forms T-shaped control markers on region boundaries.\n- Some controls use three singleton color-1 pixels around a colored center; that center is a literal replacement color.\n- An optional 5-cell L-shaped color-9 marker triggers 90-degree rotation of the output.\n\nRule:\n- For a color-1 T marker, the single stem points at the destination region to repaint.\n- The two diagonal cells behind the stem identify the source region; the destination gets that source region's original color.\n- For a colored-center T marker, the same stem identifies the destination, but the center color is the replacement.\n- All marker cells are filled with the final color of the source-side region.\n- A 5-cell L-shaped color-9 marker controls rotation: ┐ (corner top-right) → CCW 90°, ┌ (corner top-left) → CW 90°.\n\nKey insight:\n- The stem chooses what changes, while the two diagonals behind the stem choose where the marker itself belongs.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"b99e7126\" lines=\"34-48\">\n<description>\nScale a minority cell's inner pattern up to the cell-grid level, completing a fractal overlay.\n\nInput structure:\n- Grid divided into NxN cells by separator lines of a single color.\n- Most cells share an identical interior pattern (majority); a few have a distinct pattern (minority).\n\nRule:\n- Extract the inner pixel pattern from the minority cell.\n- Treat each pixel as a cell-sized stamp: active pixels → minority pattern, inactive → majority pattern.\n- Position this scaled-up pattern on the cell grid so existing minority cells align with active pixels.\n- Fill in all remaining active positions with the minority pattern.\n\nKey insight: The minority cell's interior is a miniature blueprint of where minority cells should appear at the grid scale — fractal self-similarity.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"42f83767\" lines=\"23-39\">\n<description>\nExpand the lower color grid by replacing each pixel with its matching top stencil.\n\nInput structure\nThe input has a top legend and a lower dense color grid, separated by blank rows.\nThe legend contains left-to-right 2x2 color swatches and, to their right,\nthe same number of square neutral-color stencil masks.\n\nRule\nPair the swatches and stencil masks by left-to-right order. For every pixel in\nthe lower color grid, paste the paired stencil mask as one output block,\nrecoloring the mask pixels with that pixel's color and leaving mask holes as bg.\n\nKey insight\nThe lower grid is not copied directly; it is an index grid whose colors select\nthe stencil shape from the legend, so each input pixel becomes one square block.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"79cce52d\" lines=\"29-44\">\n<description>\nCrop off the control row and column, then cyclically roll the payload by the two marker offsets.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is square, with a control row on top and a control column on the left.\n- The control row and column share one filler color and one single-pixel marker color.\n- The lower-right square is the payload and contains none of the control colors.\n\nRule\n- Remove the top control row and left control column.\n- Roll the remaining payload down by the marker's zero-based position in the left control column.\n- Roll the payload right by the marker's zero-based position in the top control row.\n\nKey insight\n- The two border markers are cyclic shift amounts for the payload, not colors to preserve.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"f560132c\" lines=\"259-273\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered jigsaw pieces into a rectangle, recoloring each by a legend sprite's color map.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several monochrome non-bg sprites (the \"pieces\"), scattered across the grid.\n- One sprite is the legend: it contains a multi-color k×k patch (typically 2×2) encoding output colors.\n\nRule:\n- Tile all pieces (rotations/reflections allowed) into a tight rectangle with no gaps.\n- The legend's k×k color patch defines a spatial color map over the rectangle.\n- Each piece is recolored to the legend-map color corresponding to its position in the assembled rectangle.\n\nKey insight: The legend piece doubles as both a jigsaw piece and a color key — its internal k×k palette assigns colors by spatial quadrant.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5a719d11\" lines=\"44-59\">\n<description>\nWithin each horizontal pair of two-color panels, swap the motif masks and recolor each pasted mask with the source panel's base color.\n\nInput structure:\n- A separator color forms full blank rows and at least one full blank column.\n- The non-separator area is split into row bands, each containing exactly two same-shaped rectangular panels.\n- Each panel has a dominant base color and one minority motif color.\n\nRule:\n- For every row band, clear each panel to its own base color.\n- Paste the right panel's motif mask into the left panel using the right base color.\n- Paste the left panel's motif mask into the right panel using the left base color.\n\nKey insight:\n- The motif colors themselves are distractors; only each motif's shape mask and the two panel base colors are exchanged.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"d35bdbdc\" lines=\"120-134\">\n<description>\nTransfer center colors between bordered sprites matched by color, keeping only path-connected ones.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, grey (5) path network, and several small \"bordered sprites\" each having a border color and a single center pixel of a different color.\n- Grey path forms a one-stroke trail under 8-connectivity; its start/end cells touch exactly two destination sprites via ports.\n\nRule:\n- Destination sprites are determined by the grey trail's two endpoints.\n- For each destination, find the source sprite whose border color equals the destination's center color.\n- Replace the destination's center pixel with the source's center color.\n- Delete all non-destination sprites; grey paths and destinations remain.\n\nKey insight: Center color of each destination acts as a lookup key into the border colors of other sprites, enabling indirect color transfer.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e3fe1151\" lines=\"36-55\">\n<description>\nFill the one blank in each corner region so all four regions have the same color multiset.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant background color forms one solid separator row and one solid\n  separator column.\n- The separator cross splits the grid into four equal rectangular corner regions.\n- Each corner region contains exactly one background-colored blank pixel.\n\nRule:\n- Count the non-background colors in each corner region.\n- The completed region multiset is defined by the maximum observed count of\n  each color across the four regions.\n- Replace each region's blank pixel with the single color needed to match that\n  completed multiset.\n\nKey insight:\n- The separator color inside a corner region is a missing value marker; comparing\n  the four regions reveals the shared multiset without using numeric color order.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"689c358e\" lines=\"72-88\">\n<description>\nMark each arrow-like sprite's pointed frame endpoint with its color and the opposite endpoint black.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a one-pixel solid rectangular frame around a dominant interior background.\n- Inside the frame are monochrome six-pixel sprites shaped as asymmetric T arrows.\n- Each sprite has a length-4 bar and a length-3 crossbar one cell closer to the pointed end.\n\nRule:\n- For each sprite, follow its bar axis from the shorter end to the frame.\n- Paint that frame endpoint with the sprite color.\n- Paint the opposite endpoint on the same row or column black.\n- Leave the interior background, sprites, and all other frame pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The direction is encoded by the crossbar's off-center position: the nearer end is the pointing end.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"eee78d87\" lines=\"40-55\">\n<description>\nDecode the colored 3x3 contour motif into a 16x16 periodic lattice with the motif stamped in maroon at the center.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is 6x6 with orange background and one non-orange color.\n- The non-orange pixels have a tight 3x3 bbox containing one contour glyph.\n- The glyph is one of the task's period-3 contour forms: plus, H, X, or four corners.\n\nRule:\n- Decode the 3x3 glyph into the period-3 orange phase tile.\n- Fill a black 16x16 canvas by repeating that orange phase tile.\n- Tile the original 3x3 glyph 2x2 and recolor its true pixels to maroon in the centered 6x6 region.\n\nKey insight:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
